[[:db/retract 17592186052755 :answer-field/choices 17592186052756] [:db/retract 17592186052755 :answer-field/choices 17592186052757] [:db/retract 17592186052755 :answer-field/choices 17592186052758] [:db/retract 17592186052755 :answer-field/choices 17592186052760] [:db/retract 17592186054057 :answer-field/choices 17592186054059] [:db/retract 17592186054057 :answer-field/choices 17592186054060] [:db/retract 17592186054057 :answer-field/choices 17592186054061] [:db/retract 17592186054057 :answer-field/choices 17592186054062] [:db/retract 17592186052737 :answer-field/choices 17592186052738] [:db/retract 17592186052737 :answer-field/choices 17592186052739] [:db/retract 17592186052737 :answer-field/choices 17592186052740] [:db/retract 17592186052737 :answer-field/choices 17592186052741] [:db/retract 17592186052737 :answer-field/choices 17592186052742] {:db/id 17592186054795 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-028210c4-ea68-52ea-a069-08f10f6556b8" "question-3cd9e82e-52f1-5f56-a2b7-86d371d48c98"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054563 :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2x-6} & =2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{2x-6})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ 2x-6 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2(5)-6} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=5$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2$  is $x=5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054564 :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{5-x} = -2$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{4-x} = 5$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{2x+1} = -1$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054565 :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5x+8}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5x+8} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5x+8})}^{2} & ={(2)}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+8 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x=4,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-5(1)+8}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}-5(4)+8}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $x=1$ and $x=4$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0$ are $x=1$ and $x=4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054798 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4cfcdd38-dccd-5180-beb8-71439df888fe"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054510 :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+10} & =x-2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{x+10})}^{2} & ={(x-2)}^{2} \\\\ x+10 & ={x}^{2}-4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x-6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=-1$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-1+10} & \\overset{?}{=}-1-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{9} & \\overset{?}{=}-3 \\\\ 3 & \\ne -3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=6$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6+10} & \\overset{?}{=}6-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=6$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of $\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2$  is $x=6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-028210c4-ea68-52ea-a069-08f10f6556b8" :question/id #uuid "028210c4-ea68-52ea-a069-08f10f6556b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-3037" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\sqrt{x-4}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x-4} & =2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{x-4})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x-4 & =4 \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8-4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{x-4}=2$  is $x=8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c1955fe9-f651-5dc5-b349-16fe6f39ca4a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1955fe9-f651-5dc5-b349-16fe6f39ca4a" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1955fe9-f651-5dc5-b349-16fe6f39ca4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9d0cb403-5d52-5f25-8a18-82eb266418a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0ce5edb2-e927-5ea3-b67d-0bf3cc56ffbb" "answer-5a03a6f4-3c36-552e-83ef-902feff74a7d" "answer-9a95c02f-bd33-5a18-a681-603645cf3866" "answer-7832e3c6-f6f7-52f5-be46-35293bf9931e" "answer-9d0cb403-5d52-5f25-8a18-82eb266418a2"] :db/ensure [: