d/choices 17592186363435] [:db/retract 17592186363431 :answer-field/choices 17592186363436] [:db/retract 17592186176398 :question/answer-fields 17592186174646] {:db/id 17592186175966 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-554c2eb9-1068-5995-9e2b-d384110d6d5e" "question-16bc20a0-cc25-520a-9947-7c91335695c8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175967 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-151bdd66-5085-5cc9-a9af-25457fe9b069" "question-f975b4e0-ae07-50ae-a2a8-6fe6de3927cf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176067 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1b4d1e3e-36ae-5b22-9e31-1455caee6c30" "question-a7ea971b-d67e-5a60-86aa-35a475db9053"] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Shifted Sphere or Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176184 :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given plane makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173362 :answer-field/correct "answer-d7436a96-8f7a-563d-8a2e-27bd76bd83af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d7436a96-8f7a-563d-8a2e-27bd76bd83af" "answer-6d48c000-d46c-5620-896f-57c4438decc4" "answer-65926222-59a9-5c6b-8e18-d6118f338b81" "answer-3ade53df-f047-5ea9-ad5f-64f2b44fc397" "answer-5dd7221e-4681-539c-8b73-08fedb80fbbf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7436a96-8f7a-563d-8a2e-27bd76bd83af" :answer/id #uuid "d7436a96-8f7a-563d-8a2e-27bd76bd83af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d48c000-d46c-5620-896f-57c4438decc4" :answer/id #uuid "6d48c000-d46c-5620-896f-57c4438decc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65926222-59a9-5c6b-8e18-d6118f338b81" :answer/id #uuid "65926222-59a9-5c6b-8e18-d6118f338b81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=\\frac{\\pi }{3}ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ade53df-f047-5ea9-ad5f-64f2b44fc397" :answer/id #uuid "3ade53df-f047-5ea9-ad5f-64f2b44fc397" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dd7221e-4681-539c-8b73-08fedb80fbbf" :answer/id #uuid "5dd7221e-4681-539c-8b73-08fedb80fbbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-554c2eb9-1068-5995-9e2b-d384110d6d5e" :question/id #uuid "554c2eb9-1068-5995-9e2b-d384110d6d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-135255" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c923ff3fd6a74263ed05a0ace025e05b0e1b5c445c2bfb0801b8707ce9ac356.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-77d22858-0f2e-526e-bced-96022129988c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-77d22858-0f2e-526e-bced-96022129988c" :answer-field/id #uuid "77d22858-0f2e-526e-bced-96022129988c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-533da413-4cc6-5072-bfd4-5130aa7b1b4c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d379e0ab-40d7-5be4-a02e-62f11a49df2c" "answer-95019874-835f-5630-8478-7510f7ff01d5" "answer-d7f9704c-1b98-5e60-a1d1-c90ca30c1a6d" "answer-d9f36413-282b-594b-bf19-b41c6ca3f9d8" "answer-533da413-4cc6-5072-bfd4-5130aa7b1b4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d379e0ab-40d7-5be4-a02e-62f11a49df2c" :answer/id #uuid "d379e0ab-40d7-5be4-a02e-62f11a49df2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho =\\frac{\\theta }{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95019874-835f-5630-8478-7510f7ff01d5" :answer/id #uuid "95019874-835f-5630-8478-7510f7ff01d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7f9704c-1b98-5e60-a1d1-c90ca30c1a6d" :answer/id #uuid "d7f9704c-1b98-5e60-a1d1-c90ca30c1a6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9f36413-282b-594b-bf19-b41c6ca3f9d8" :answer/id #uuid "d9f36413-282b-594b-bf19-b41c6ca3f9d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-533da413-4cc6-5072-bfd4-5130aa7b1b4c" :answer/id #uuid "533da413-4cc6-5072-bfd4-5130aa7b1b4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-16bc20a0-cc25-520a-9947-7c91335695c8" :question/id #uuid "16bc20a0-cc25-520a-9947-7c91335695c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-135293" :question/problem "![](images/91/917233475c847130eba1875f80606d91b03b53517fdb9273a3b32502619ef522.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\frac{8\\pi }{9}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $ϕ=\\frac{8\\pi }{9}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7c0bc77f-96c8-5f31-bddf-b61325129b9a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7c0bc77f-96c8-5f31-bddf-b61325129b9a" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c0bc77f-96c8-5f31-bddf-b61325129b9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cf78c873-e6cf-5b6f-a593-5a1a0064a21b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a095849-30a2-5a86-b94a-5937c6527642" "answer-c5446027-bf25-5b95-b225-a1cfb71d17e0" "answer-146aa21f-44ca-51df-b0da-ac4f658fac27" "answer-cf78c873-e6cf-5b6f-a593-5a1a0064a21b" "answer-f8c22772-3e88-5eda-bc0a-b400e08d3084"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a095849-30a2-5a86-b94a-5937c6527642" :answer/id #uuid "7a095849-30a2-5a86-b94a-5937c6527642" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho =\\frac{8\\pi }{9}ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5446027-bf25-5b95-b225-a1cfb71d17e0" :answer/id #uuid "c5446027-bf25-5b95-b225-a1cfb71d17e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{8\\pi }{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-146aa21f-44ca-51df-b0da-ac4f658fac27" :answer/id #uuid "146aa21f-44ca-51df-b0da-ac4f658fac27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf78c873-e6cf-5b6f-a593-5a1a0064a21b" :answer/id #uuid "cf78c873-e6cf-5b6f-a593-5a1a0064a21b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{8\\pi }{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8c22772-3e88-5eda-bc0a-b400e08d3084" :answer/id #uuid "f8c22772-3e88-5eda-bc0a-b400e08d3084" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-151bdd66-5085-5cc9-a9af-25457fe9b069" :question/id #uuid "151bdd66-5085-5cc9-a9af-25457fe9b069" :question/math-academy-id "q-135301" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\pi $ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\pi \n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $\\pi .$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](images/41/415596385e885cf6c8fb553d126c550a78879d3503267bae102a8e038a688eb3.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89d19a91-9b5e-5bde-be4d-876637ef2257"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89d19a91-9b5e-5bde-be4d-876637ef2257" :answer-field/id #uuid "89d19a91-9b5e-5bde-be4d-876637ef2257" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c3b9ed22-ede1-58b1-b1b0-d97a6798e3e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3b9ed22-ede1-58b1-b1b0-d97a6798e3e7" "answer-3d59c90c-9f40-57a5-8e98-bfd01f70ea85" "answer-3c27e55e-48dd-567a-b8e9-f381889e732a" "answer-479a1efc-6aee-5db2-81fe-fdda094e2d95" "answer-32461572-55bc-5f57-b231-fb17b4eaa812"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3b9ed22-ede1-58b1-b1b0-d97a6798e3e7" :answer/id #uuid "c3b9ed22-ede1-58b1-b1b0-d97a6798e3e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A half-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d59c90c-9f40-57a5-8e98-bfd01f70ea85" :answer/id #uuid "3d59c90c-9f40-57a5-8e98-bfd01f70ea85" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A hyperboloid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c27e55e-48dd-567a-b8e9-f381889e732a" :answer/id #uuid "3c27e55e-48dd-567a-b8e9-f381889e732a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A line" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-479a1efc-6aee-5db2-81fe-fdda094e2d95" :answer/id #uuid "479a1efc-6aee-5db2-81fe-fdda094e2d95" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32461572-55bc-5f57-b231-fb17b4eaa812" :answer/id #uuid "32461572-55bc-5f57-b231-fb17b4eaa812" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A sphere" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f975b4e0-ae07-50ae-a2a8-6fe6de3927cf" :question/id #uuid "f975b4e0-ae07-50ae-a2a8-6fe6de3927cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-135297" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho =1$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho =1\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $1.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere.\n\n![](images/6b/6bca743f355735dae56d679cc601852f5a52bd5cc99e89a8d4f20a92111cf621.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0f41120f-43ad-516f-be5d-f4fc7aad71f6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0f41120f-43ad-516f-be5d-f4fc7aad71f6" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f41120f-43ad-516f-be5d-f4fc7aad71f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b7434448-9bab-5c7b-8c21-bca12a9fa684" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2e47c97e-55dc-53c8-9711-65d1b9fc5ec9" "answer-2fed5099-caf7-543c-92f2-49d96f84c06e" "answer-a698c164-580d-54d0-9ef9-6f4e0a0b42c6" "answer-31aa13bc-974f-57ab-9747-45bd823fbe35" "answer-b7434448-9bab-5c7b-8c21-bca12a9fa684"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e47c97e-55dc-53c8-9711-65d1b9fc5ec9" :answer/id #uuid "2e47c97e-55dc-53c8-9711-65d1b9fc5ec9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A cone" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fed5099-caf7-543c-92f2-49d96f84c06e" :answer/id #uuid "2fed5099-caf7-543c-92f2-49d96f84c06e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a698c164-580d-54d0-9ef9-6f4e0a0b42c6" :answer/id #uuid "a698c164-580d-54d0-9ef9-6f4e0a0b42c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31aa13bc-974f-57ab-9747-45bd823fbe35" :answer/id #uuid "31aa13bc-974f-57ab-9747-45bd823fbe35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7434448-9bab-5c7b-8c21-bca12a9fa684" :answer/id #uuid "b7434448-9bab-5c7b-8c21-bca12a9fa684" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A sphere" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281346 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/6c/6cf95f58e436577be5ad696325ae2d1e7bfd5882b4899e88806c4ae653a5c518.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,6]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi /2]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186280968 :answer-field/correct "answer-608156a9-23aa-57d6-b271-1637a899487d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4faf5c03-6510-5ce8-addf-575bb84d8f13" "answer-f4ba179b-d2b8-58c4-9e9b-66578364394c" "answer-608156a9-23aa-57d6-b271-1637a899487d" "answer-bb1d01c9-8cd3-51c4-9e7c-da21a084bab7" "answer-720b34ff-283b-595f-8f21-a06e203e3f30"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4faf5c03-6510-5ce8-addf-575bb84d8f13" :answer/id #uuid "4faf5c03-6510-5ce8-addf-575bb84d8f13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 12,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /6\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4ba179b-d2b8-58c4-9e9b-66578364394c" :answer/id #uuid "f4ba179b-d2b8-58c4-9e9b-66578364394c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 36,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-608156a9-23aa-57d6-b271-1637a899487d" :answer/id #uuid "608156a9-23aa-57d6-b271-1637a899487d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb1d01c9-8cd3-51c4-9e7c-da21a084bab7" :answer/id #uuid "bb1d01c9-8cd3-51c4-9e7c-da21a084bab7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /4\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-720b34ff-283b-595f-8f21-a06e203e3f30" :answer/id #uuid "720b34ff-283b-595f-8f21-a06e203e3f30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281349 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/cc/cca15d8d91ebfe643777122cf41a49f2bfe3bac901b30e160cad65eb6b4b102f.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0