ue "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec5d739d-c5b9-59ad-949f-5db65c190d29" :answer/id #uuid "ec5d739d-c5b9-59ad-949f-5db65c190d29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233071 :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0f914387-42f8-52dc-abdc-1d71f70ce1d4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0f914387-42f8-52dc-abdc-1d71f70ce1d4" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f914387-42f8-52dc-abdc-1d71f70ce1d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d2dd366d-1802-52fa-b798-868adb32b32e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7cdf0be9-e0e1-56db-82b8-4ccd9f5bdbc6" "answer-d22b8ac3-c049-5865-9013-008bea9a7215" "answer-c25b4b4a-273b-533d-872e-af47c9ed53fb" "answer-3cba47ec-7248-5b5f-b8bb-800bfc3a0781" "answer-d2dd366d-1802-52fa-b798-868adb32b32e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cdf0be9-e0e1-56db-82b8-4ccd9f5bdbc6" :answer/id #uuid "7cdf0be9-e0e1-56db-82b8-4ccd9f5bdbc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d22b8ac3-c049-5865-9013-008bea9a7215" :answer/id #uuid "d22b8ac3-c049-5865-9013-008bea9a7215" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c25b4b4a-273b-533d-872e-af47c9ed53fb" :answer/id #uuid "c25b4b4a-273b-533d-872e-af47c9ed53fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cba47ec-7248-5b5f-b8bb-800bfc3a0781" :answer/id #uuid "3cba47ec-7248-5b5f-b8bb-800bfc3a0781" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=\\pm 3600" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2dd366d-1802-52fa-b798-868adb32b32e" :answer/id #uuid "d2dd366d-1802-52fa-b798-868adb32b32e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=\\pm 60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-10d98fee-4a02-5f93-813f-e8d53425cb38" :question/id #uuid "10d98fee-4a02-5f93-813f-e8d53425cb38" :question/math-academy-id "q-44577" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $3{x}^{2}+12x+k=0$ has two distinct real solutions." :question/worked-solution "The equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is positive.\n\nWe have $a=3,$ $b=12,$ and $c=k,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={12}^{2}-4(3)(k) \\\\  & =144-12k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & >0 \\\\ 144-12k & >0 \\\\ 144 & >12k \\\\ k & <12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have two distinct real solutions if $k<12.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f9816bbe-93b2-52b6-837d-9867d4d1aa47"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f9816bbe-93b2-52b6-837d-9867d4d1aa47" :answer-field/id #uuid "f9816bbe-93b2-52b6-837d-9867d4d1aa47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b51b432d-5224-5995-95d9-0472ce8e9b88" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b51b432d-5224-5995-95d9-0472ce8e9b88" "answer-55f4fc92-aa88-5239-b750-208704cac86e" "answer-183b332e-d73d-5559-aad9-bd1b300be86c" "answer-0bc16e37-ecee-5679-a64a-218fee94f795" "answer-b7dcd89c-6bd2-5e12-8812-c82738dd636d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b51b432d-5224-5995-95d9-0472ce8e9b88" :answer/id #uuid "b51b432d-5224-5995-95d9-0472ce8e9b88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55f4fc92-aa88-5239-b750-208704cac86e" :answer/id #uuid "55f4fc92-aa88-5239-b750-208704cac86e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k<6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-183b332e-d73d-5559-aad9-bd1b300be86c" :answer/id #uuid "183b332e-d73d-5559-aad9-bd1b300be86c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bc16e37-ecee-5679-a64a-218fee94f795" :answer/id #uuid "0bc16e37-ecee-5679-a64a-218fee94f795" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k>6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7dcd89c-6bd2-5e12-8812-c82738dd636d" :answer/id #uuid "b7dcd89c-6bd2-5e12-8812-c82738dd636d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k\\ge 15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d9f98f1e-4801-5a10-97a0-297033319c24" :question/id #uuid "d9f98f1e-4801-5a10-97a0-297033319c24" :question/math-academy-id "q-158008" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation $2{x}^{2}-2x-2=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $a=2,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=-2.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(2)(-2) \\\\  & =4+16 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $20$ is *not* a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true.\n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is not a perfect square, the equation must have two irrational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-11f49b58-68ca-5023-ae7f-9ab77db5a13c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-11f49b58-68ca-5023-ae7f-9ab77db5a13c" :answer-field/id #uuid "11f49b58-68ca-5023-ae7f-9ab77db5a13c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-870ca322-0deb-526f-8d2a-237cf9b7375c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fabeb6ac-b3cb-5d6b-a30c-ac5b939b9949" "answer-1ca03129-5630-59ae-856c-9aeac68e2746" "answer-8a985eb6-e820-5437-81f1-28b8193dc517" "answer-0e90531f-10e7-5a64-a37d-2ff16fbe4573" "answer-870ca322-0deb-526f-8d2a-237cf9b7375c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fabeb6ac-b3cb-5d6b-a30c-ac5b939b9949" :answer/id #uuid "fabeb6ac-b3cb-5d6b-a30c-ac5b939b9949" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ca03129-5630-59ae-856c-9aeac68e2746" :answer/id #uuid "1ca03129-5630-59ae-856c-9aeac68e2746" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a985eb6-e820-5437-81f1-28b8193dc517" :answer/id #uuid "8a985eb6-e820-5437-81f1-28b8193dc517" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e90531f-10e7-5a64-a37d-2ff16fbe4573" :answer/id #uuid "0e90531f-10e7-5a64-a37d-2ff16fbe4573" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-870ca322-0deb-526f-8d2a-237cf9b7375c" :answer/id #uuid "870ca322-0deb-526f-8d2a-237cf9b7375c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-389f1f33-a04d-5409-a2d5-8e337f759108" :question/id #uuid "389f1f33-a04d-5409-a2d5-8e337f759108" :question/math-academy-id "q-158003" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation $4{x}^{2}+6x+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $a=4,$ $b=6,$ and $c=1.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(6)^{2}-4(4)(1) \\\\  & =36-16 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $20$ is *not* a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true.\n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is not a perfect square, the equation must have two irrational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f2d3e179-11b4-52f5-bce3-6a401512952d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f2d3e179-11b4-52f5-bce3-6a401512952d" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2d3e179-11b4-52f5-bce3-6a401512952d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ac677453-135e-55a6-afd4-f9d0634419e1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fbb0fa8a-aae6-5dd3-871e-f62b0a64d439" "answer-38183de3-6469-5b98-995c-7988edd9d1b4" "answer-2f161efc-27e4-5e36-bbe3-20a7ed6e5cbf" "answer-0d9f5221-4063-5d13-89d0-9cbeae6dfa3d" "answer-ac677453-135e-55a6-afd4-f9d0634419e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbb0fa8a-aae6-5dd3-871e-f62b0a64d439" :answer/id #uuid "fbb0fa8a-aae6-5dd3-871e-f62b0a64d439" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38183de3-6469-5b98-995c-7988edd9d1b4" :answer/id #uuid "38183de3-6469-5b98-995c-7988edd9d1b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f161efc-27e4-5e36-bbe3-20a7ed6e5cbf" :answer/id #uuid "2f161efc-27e4-5e36-bbe3-20a7ed6e5cbf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d9f5221-4063-5d13-89d0-9cbeae6dfa3d" :answer/id #uuid "0d9f5221-4063-5d13-89d0-9cbeae6dfa3d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac677453-135e-55a6-afd4-f9d0634419e1" :answer/id #uuid "ac677453-135e-55a6-afd4-f9d0634419e1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069996 :tutorial/content "Given a quadratic equation in the form \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0,\n$$\n\nwe can find its roots using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this formula, the expression under the square root is called the **discriminant**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = {\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}\n$$\n\nThe value of the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ tells us a lot about the solutions of our quadratic equation.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}>0,$ then we get $2$ distinct real solutions: one with the positive square root and one with the negative square root.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}=0,$ then we get $1$ real solution because the square root vanishes. We sometimes say that the equation has two equal solutions.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}<0,$ then we get no real solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples:\n\n- If $x^2+3x+2=0,$ then we have a positive discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=3^2-4(1)(2) = 1$ and $2$ distinct real solutions:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{3^2-4(1)(2)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{1} }{2} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm 1}{2} = -2,-1\n$$\n\n- If $x^2+2x+1=0,$ then we have a zero discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=2^2-4(1)(1) = 0$ and $1$ distinct real solution:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(1)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{0} }{2} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm 0}{2} = -1\n$$\n\n- If $x^2+x+1=0,$ then we have a negative discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=1^2-4(1)(1) = -3$ and no real solutions:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2-4(1)(1)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3} }{2}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069997 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the quadratic equation \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the coefficients $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are all *rational numbers,* and\n\n- the discriminant is non-negative, i.e., $\\mathcal D \\geq 0.$\n\nThen, we have the following possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is a *perfect square*, then the equation has two *rational* solutions.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is *not* a perfect square, then the equation has two *irrational* solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples.\n\nConsider the equation $x^2-x-6=0.$ Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(-6) \\\\  & =1+24 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a perfect square. Consequently, the corresponding solutions are both rational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{5^2}}{2(1)} = 3, -2\n$$\n\nNow, consider the equation $x^2-x-5=0.$ Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(-5) \\\\  & =1+20 \\\\  & =21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a perfect square. Consequently, the solutions are both irrational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{21}}{2}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068958 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133873 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9cceaafc-6aa8-5022-9186-b46a1e5cf4cd" "question-37c6cb32-2e5c-597b-a1f3-350655b79559"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186240962 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{g(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}+2)\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{5}+50{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+20{t}^{2}+2)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{6}{t}^{6}+10{t}^{5}+\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{20}{3}{t}^{3}+2t]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{6}+10+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{20}{3}+2)-(\\frac{5}{6}-10+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{20}{3}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(25{t}^{4}+10{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[5{t}^{5}+\\frac{10}{3}{t}^{3}+t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(5+\\frac{10}{3}+1)-(-5-\\frac{10}{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{112}{3})}{(\\frac{56}{3})}\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2(5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =10{t}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9cceaafc-6aa8-5022-9186-b46a1e5cf4cd" :question/id #uuid "9cceaafc-6aa8-5022-9186-b46a1e5cf4cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-104579" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=4{t}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{q(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2}){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot (t\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+(4{t}^{2}){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot (t\\,-\\,1){|}_{1}+(4{t}^{2}){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot (t\\,-\\,1){|}_{2} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 0+16\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+0+16 \\\\  & =16,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)+q(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(t\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot (t\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+(t\\,-\\,1){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot (t\\,-\\,1){|}_{1}+(t\\,-\\,1){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot (t\\,-\\,1){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+0+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =8(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b4556a5d-cb8e-58d6-984f-3172a7d4565b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4556a5d-cb8e-58d6-984f-3172a7d4565b" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4556a5d-cb8e-58d6-984f-3172a7d4565b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6686dbbc-8385-5ed8-a939-8f58388fdefc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e6e143cf-f1e1-51bb-b77c-9575604dcb48" "answer-14b14a05-4fc6-5596-8368-6ba28a96940f" "answer-1712a6a4-0289-5d1b-a0b4-24b472a45d9f" "answer-6686dbbc-8385-5ed8-a939-8f58388fdefc" "answer-4b296421-8574-5316-bf14-f69e5d1a35cf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6e143cf-f1e1-51bb-b77c-9575604dcb48" :answer/id #uuid "e6e143cf-f1e1-51bb-b77c-9575604dcb48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16(t-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14b14a05-4fc6-5596-8368-6ba28a96940f" :answer/id #uuid "14b14a05-4fc6-5596-8368-6ba28a96940f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1712a6a4-0289-5d1b-a0b4-24b472a45d9f" :answer/id #uuid "1712a6a4-0289-5d1b-a0b4-24b472a45d9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4(t-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6686dbbc-8385-5ed8-a939-8f58388fdefc" :answer/id #uuid "6686dbbc-8385-5ed8-a939-8f58388fdefc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8(t-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b296421-8574-5316-bf14-f69e5d1a35cf" :answer/id #uuid "4b296421-8574-5316-bf14-f69e5d1a35cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186240903 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{q(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(5{t}^{2}){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}+(5{t}^{2}){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}+(5{t}^{2}){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+0+10 \\\\  & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}+(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}+(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =2(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186240904 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{g(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2}+12t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[3{t}^{4}-8{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-8+6)-0 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-37c6cb32-2e5c-597b-a1f3-350655b79559" :question/id #uuid "37c6cb32-2e5c-597b-a1f3-350655b79559" :question/math-academy-id "q-104914" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[-1,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=12{t}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=3t-3.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{g(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}12{t}^{2}\\cdot (3t-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(36{t}^{3}-36{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[9{t}^{4}-12{t}^{3}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(9-12)-(9+12) \\\\  & =-24,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-18t+9)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[3{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+9t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-9+9)-(-3-9-9) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{-24}{24}\\cdot (3t-3) \\\\  & =-3t+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2247c9a9-9f6c-5603-a7f0-e48ee4cd2791"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2247c9a9-9f6c-5603-a7f0-e48ee4cd2791" :answer-field/id #uuid "2247c9a9-9f6c-5603-a7f0-e48ee4cd2791" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d7dcc5fc-8619-5718-afee-e09b0b6fc0b3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d7dcc5fc-8619-5718-afee-e09b0b6fc0b3" "answer-ea750627-9116-50ab-8739-12be8ee7180b" "answer-361c1889-55a9-56a7-aee6-7f570e3670a1" "answer-f422659d-f13f-565d-9061-06dd402dc1fa" "answer-83e6d1e6-0706-50bb-a71e-b16fbedec186"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7dcc5fc-8619-5718-afee-e09b0b6fc0b3" :answer/id #uuid "d7dcc5fc-8619-5718-afee-e09b0b6fc0b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3t+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea750627-9116-50ab-8739-12be8ee7180b" :answer/id #uuid "ea750627-9116-50ab-8739-12be8ee7180b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-t+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-361c1889-55a9-56a7-aee6-7f570e3670a1" :answer/id #uuid "361c1889-55a9-56a7-aee6-7f570e3670a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f422659d-f13f-565d-9061-06dd402dc1fa" :answer/id #uuid "f422659d-f13f-565d-9061-06dd402dc1fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3t-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83e6d1e6-0706-50bb-a71e-b16fbedec186" :answer/id #uuid "83e6d1e6-0706-50bb-a71e-b16fbedec186" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186240950 :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $1,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-1,\\,q(t)=4t-3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t)\\}$ equals $t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{2}(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(4t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(4t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}{|}_{0}+(t-1)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1)=3t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =t+(3t-3) \\\\  & =4t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186241003 :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t)\\}$ equals $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{2}(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+6{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have integrals over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186240983 :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t)\\}$ equals $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{2}(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186240978 :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$ \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=3t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}+1,\\,g(t)=10{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{1}(t)\\}$ equals $6t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{f}_{2}(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $10{t}^{3}$ is an odd function, while ${t}^{2}+1$ is even. Hence, their product $10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6t+0 \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186240955 :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $2,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,q(t)={t}^{2}+t\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ equals $0.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=0,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩ & =q(-1){p}_{1}(-1)+q(0){p}_{1}(0)+q(1){p}_{1}(1) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{1}(-1){p}_{1}(-1)+{p}_{1}(0){p}_{1}(0)+{p}_{1}(1){p}_{1}(1) \\\\  & =(t)^{2}{|}_{-1}+(t)^{2}{|}_{0}+(t)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot t=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =t+0 \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186241065 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{q(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in \\{-1,0,1\\}}(5t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in \\{-1,0,1\\}}(t-1){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 0}{(-2)\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =2(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=5t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖5t-2(t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in \\{-1,0,1\\}}(3t+2){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (3t+2){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =1+4+25 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{30}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186241074 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{g(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}7t\\cdot (t-2)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{t}^{2}-14t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-4t+4)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{[\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}-7{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1}}{[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{14}{3})}{(\\frac{7}{3})}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =-2t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=7t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-2$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖7t-(-2t+4)‖ \\\\  & =‖9t-4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨9t-4,9t-4⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨9t-4,9t-4⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9t-4)\\cdot (9t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9t-4)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(81{t}^{2}-72t+16)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(27{t}^{3}-36{t}^{2}+16)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{7}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186375652 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{q(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in \\{-1,0,1\\}}(5{t}^{2}-5){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in \\{-1,0,1\\}}(t+1){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 0+(-5)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2}{0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{5}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =-t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=5{t}^{2}-5$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t+1$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖5{t}^{2}-5-(-t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖5{t}^{2}+t-4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨5{t}^{2}+t-4,5{t}^{2}+t-4⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨5{t}^{2}+t-4,5{t}^{2}+t-4⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in \\{-1,0,1\\}}(5{t}^{2}+t-4){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+t-4){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =(0)\\cdot (0)+(-4)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+16+4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{20}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134005 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172090 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ef442bea-bb64-52fe-bd5a-d969e4e9ca72"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ef442bea-bb64-52fe-bd5a-d969e4e9ca72" :question/id #uuid "ef442bea-bb64-52fe-bd5a-d969e4e9ca72" :question/math-academy-id "q-123558" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c27ab9b0062fabfe1fdbfd43631bf1f143c0fa4ec80587519e9ce6c4e84e7b2b.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}-\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b4d67ad9-bea8-55c4-a76f-57659a7c98ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4d67ad9-bea8-55c4-a76f-57659a7c98ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d67ad9-bea8-55c4-a76f-57659a7c98ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cbde0e1b-dda5-5de9-b0d5-ad1180eecbdf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-61bbc39f-0989-5704-9418-422cadcf22ca" "answer-cbde0e1b-dda5-5de9-b0d5-ad1180eecbdf" "answer-91cf89e0-afa8-591c-bc93-ee02e4978fb5" "answer-bd3eced4-b5ba-5283-97ad-820c6e28c0ec" "answer-3de248ef-eae0-53e3-95b5-fbeb5926dd60"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61bbc39f-0989-5704-9418-422cadcf22ca" :answer/id #uuid "61bbc39f-0989-5704-9418-422cadcf22ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbde0e1b-dda5-5de9-b0d5-ad1180eecbdf" :answer/id #uuid "cbde0e1b-dda5-5de9-b0d5-ad1180eecbdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91cf89e0-afa8-591c-bc93-ee02e4978fb5" :answer/id #uuid "91cf89e0-afa8-591c-bc93-ee02e4978fb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd3eced4-b5ba-5283-97ad-820c6e28c0ec" :answer/id #uuid "bd3eced4-b5ba-5283-97ad-820c6e28c0ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3de248ef-eae0-53e3-95b5-fbeb5926dd60" :answer/id #uuid "3de248ef-eae0-53e3-95b5-fbeb5926dd60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-822bcaa0-c52b-52f8-b781-cc9986a54dc7" :activity/id #uuid "822bcaa0-c52b-52f8-b781-cc9986a54dc7" :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049041 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-d5675ffb-cacc-5ee5-aa57-add6376682b0" "step-1b608b4b-9e61-5390-845a-6b9f724dc703" "step-87995df5-9cb4-59d7-9813-d9c7cc1db2d9" "step-cd77c6be-b59a-50ba-9fc2-79d740821700" "step-f7f86389-8351-5a11-bac8-76649d974f77"] :activity/first-step "step-d5675ffb-cacc-5ee5-aa57-add6376682b0" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d5675ffb-cacc-5ee5-aa57-add6376682b0" :step/id #uuid "d5675ffb-cacc-5ee5-aa57-add6376682b0" :step/content 17592186172288 :step/next "step-1b608b4b-9e61-5390-845a-6b9f724dc703" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1b608b4b-9e61-5390-845a-6b9f724dc703" :step/id #uuid "1b608b4b-9e61-5390-845a-6b9f724dc703" :step/content 17592186447154 :step/next "step-87995df5-9cb4-59d7-9813-d9c7cc1db2d9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-87995df5-9cb4-59d7-9813-d9c7cc1db2d9" :step/id #uuid "87995df5-9cb4-59d7-9813-d9c7cc1db2d9" :step/content 17592186483367 :step/next "step-cd77c6be-b59a-50ba-9fc2-79d740821700" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cd77c6be-b59a-50ba-9fc2-79d740821700" :step/id #uuid "cd77c6be-b59a-50ba-9fc2-79d740821700" :step/content 17592186172289 :step/next "step-f7f86389-8351-5a11-bac8-76649d974f77" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f7f86389-8351-5a11-bac8-76649d974f77" :step/id #uuid "f7f86389-8351-5a11-bac8-76649d974f77" :step/content "question-ef442bea-bb64-52fe-bd5a-d969e4e9ca72" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129153 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4f653225-d77d-586c-90af-460ddba800f1" "question-2ac1ab3d-25c7-532c-bff1-4864fc864532"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128971 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt x{:}$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph.\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n\nOf the given options, only graph II has all of the above properties.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is only II." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129152 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3728e5e1-e988-5755-b263-7b61d69b65af"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128970 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x) =\\sqrt{x}?$\n\nI. $f(x)$ is always positive.\n\nII. The range of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty).$\n\nIII. $f(x) \\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt x.$\n\n![](images/f8/f8825f71f4d3565b28198b3a4de5424693adfead87a509f38ffecfbccb9fcd01.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $f(0)=0 \\not\\gt 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false since the range of $f(x)$ is $[0,\\infty).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x) \\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f653225-d77d-586c-90af-460ddba800f1" :question/id #uuid "4f653225-d77d-586c-90af-460ddba800f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-103612" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d88a8e80d3008450a3e807750ee0f02c9b733eca5407d7dda416ce43303ba18.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph III has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c3500379-6a8e-53c3-8126-dfaf0c74ce0c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c3500379-6a8e-53c3-8126-dfaf0c74ce0c" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3500379-6a8e-53c3-8126-dfaf0c74ce0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f117c591-83e0-575b-b3d6-0ba013f899e3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-942918c4-468b-5f0b-abfa-42482f89f311" "answer-e3700dee-e94d-5006-be1c-e817b9ecee24" "answer-f117c591-83e0-575b-b3d6-0ba013f899e3" "answer-56286bb0-3805-550e-a429-f2ebc31c912b" "answer-16aabd13-9f25-55f2-94cd-eef26792b4a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-942918c4-468b-5f0b-abfa-42482f89f311" :answer/id #uuid "942918c4-468b-5f0b-abfa-42482f89f311" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3700dee-e94d-5006-be1c-e817b9ecee24" :answer/id #uuid "e3700dee-e94d-5006-be1c-e817b9ecee24" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f117c591-83e0-575b-b3d6-0ba013f899e3" :answer/id #uuid "f117c591-83e0-575b-b3d6-0ba013f899e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56286bb0-3805-550e-a429-f2ebc31c912b" :answer/id #uuid "56286bb0-3805-550e-a429-f2ebc31c912b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16aabd13-9f25-55f2-94cd-eef26792b4a8" :answer/id #uuid "16aabd13-9f25-55f2-94cd-eef26792b4a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the given functions is the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ac1ab3d-25c7-532c-bff1-4864fc864532" :question/id #uuid "2ac1ab3d-25c7-532c-bff1-4864fc864532" :question/math-academy-id "q-103617" :question/problem "![](images/00/00b1f319a2ac35014f0db9fc37d03069328752bc6f2f5b8369e7f8cac6bb23d0.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nNone of the given options have all of the above properties. Therefore, none of the given functions is the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f7b6939-8c77-5f6f-9a80-02e48e4bab3f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f7b6939-8c77-5f6f-9a80-02e48e4bab3f" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f7b6939-8c77-5f6f-9a80-02e48e4bab3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-07e8a5c2-6928-5951-89ad-12fdcaaff1e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-61965896-9ca7-566e-8b10-7912fe172307" "answer-fc790b2c-493c-59f5-9ba7-680583e7f01d" "answer-f31c787c-aac9-5106-af87-d156f8c85354" "answer-a3797683-6e07-5f6f-a326-b1bf5d221fbc" "answer-07e8a5c2-6928-5951-89ad-12fdcaaff1e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61965896-9ca7-566e-8b10-7912fe172307" :answer/id #uuid "61965896-9ca7-566e-8b10-7912fe172307" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc790b2c-493c-59f5-9ba7-680583e7f01d" :answer/id #uuid "fc790b2c-493c-59f5-9ba7-680583e7f01d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f31c787c-aac9-5106-af87-d156f8c85354" :answer/id #uuid "f31c787c-aac9-5106-af87-d156f8c85354" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3797683-6e07-5f6f-a326-b1bf5d221fbc" :answer/id #uuid "a3797683-6e07-5f6f-a326-b1bf5d221fbc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07e8a5c2-6928-5951-89ad-12fdcaaff1e7" :answer/id #uuid "07e8a5c2-6928-5951-89ad-12fdcaaff1e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the given functions is the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3728e5e1-e988-5755-b263-7b61d69b65af" :question/id #uuid "3728e5e1-e988-5755-b263-7b61d69b65af" :question/math-academy-id "q-89863" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}?$\n\nI. The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$\n\nII. The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\nIII. The graph of $f(x)$ lies in the fourth quadrant" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](images/6d/6dc32799c53b03cb53d692054cc44910d252367ebf102c53472d4e4d2fe78684.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(0)=\\sqrt{0}=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false since $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is defined for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false, since the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ddc9a67-b8a9-5f6a-95f8-025303a9f3ff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ddc9a67-b8a9-5f6a-95f8-025303a9f3ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ddc9a67-b8a9-5f6a-95f8-025303a9f3ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-beffdb55-5558-5125-8a6c-7a5b7f6df530" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4e3d87a2-8760-5f0f-916c-e4a3f5cb6b20" "answer-63ddf929-b194-573f-a0a6-4e5769f96516" "answer-beffdb55-5558-5125-8a6c-7a5b7f6df530" "answer-7a70cbd2-63c4-5c03-a2d4-de215207d4ca" "answer-902f594a-181d-520f-a1f9-bd315d9947b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e3d87a2-8760-5f0f-916c-e4a3f5cb6b20" :answer/id #uuid "4e3d87a2-8760-5f0f-916c-e4a3f5cb6b20" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63ddf929-b194-573f-a0a6-4e5769f96516" :answer/id #uuid "63ddf929-b194-573f-a0a6-4e5769f96516" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-beffdb55-5558-5125-8a6c-7a5b7f6df530" :answer/id #uuid "beffdb55-5558-5125-8a6c-7a5b7f6df530" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a70cbd2-63c4-5c03-a2d4-de215207d4ca" :answer/id #uuid "7a70cbd2-63c4-5c03-a2d4-de215207d4ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-902f594a-181d-520f-a1f9-bd315d9947b4" :answer/id #uuid "902f594a-181d-520f-a1f9-bd315d9947b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129747 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}?$\n\nI. The point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$\n\nII. The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\nIII. The graph of $f(x)$ lies in the second quadrant" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](images/f8/f8825f71f4d3565b28198b3a4de5424693adfead87a509f38ffecfbccb9fcd01.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $f(1)=\\sqrt{1}=1\\ne 2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is defined for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false since the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186127963 :answer-field/correct 17592186127964 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130369 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ First, we create a table of values.\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nWe do not consider negative values for the input because the square root of a negative number is not a real number.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/39/39c60b7e997c4af7b823796c0b94144572e7bff1be083564ab9fa29fd66d4a42.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(1,1)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n- The domain  is $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The range  is $y\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129376 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064289 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-94f0b932-fd80-552e-9cee-96a821a87f03"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-94f0b932-fd80-552e-9cee-96a821a87f03" :question/id #uuid "94f0b932-fd80-552e-9cee-96a821a87f03" :question/math-academy-id "q-50100" :question/problem "![](images/77/778cc96e20eead41160c37b8a39373d789c166a9d516a70591f7ba7b315f3fbf.png)\n\nWhich expression represents the total finite area enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{4}-{x}^{2}$ and $y={x}^{3}-x,$ as shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=y={x}^{4}-{x}^{2}$ and $g(x)={x}^{3}-x.$ First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{4}-{x}^{2} & ={x}^{3}-x \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{2}-1) & =x({x}^{2}-1) \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/d8/d84bd9bccba15a91ab54ee0dc64fb344c191384a14ee5d5c2fb857dc18fc63db.png)\n\nWe have:\n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $x\\in [-1,0],$ and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[g(x)-f(x)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[({x}^{3}-x)-({x}^{4}-{x}^{2})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $x\\in [0,1],$ and the upper function is $f(x)$ while the lower function is $g(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[f(x)-g(x)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[({x}^{4}-{x}^{2})-({x}^{3}-x)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fbc7a5d4-412e-593d-a67c-38cb29a7b665"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fbc7a5d4-412e-593d-a67c-38cb29a7b665" :answer-field/id #uuid "fbc7a5d4-412e-593d-a67c-38cb29a7b665" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-55668ec2-5826-5d6a-8956-9daa48c55890" :answer-field/choices ["answer-55668ec2-5826-5d6a-8956-9daa48c55890" "answer-2d10487d-2d9d-56d7-9cdc-96a964a7af60" "answer-50f305d3-6119-5d45-a72e-f8145c2fdc0a" "answer-7745504f-0ea1-5f16-a5f1-5c985b944340" "answer-da418d57-2468-5fdc-87b4-fdc98be18a66"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55668ec2-5826-5d6a-8956-9daa48c55890" :answer/id #uuid "55668ec2-5826-5d6a-8956-9daa48c55890" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d10487d-2d9d-56d7-9cdc-96a964a7af60" :answer/id #uuid "2d10487d-2d9d-56d7-9cdc-96a964a7af60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{4}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50f305d3-6119-5d45-a72e-f8145c2fdc0a" :answer/id #uuid "50f305d3-6119-5d45-a72e-f8145c2fdc0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{x}^{4}-x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7745504f-0ea1-5f16-a5f1-5c985b944340" :answer/id #uuid "7745504f-0ea1-5f16-a5f1-5c985b944340" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da418d57-2468-5fdc-87b4-fdc98be18a66" :answer/id #uuid "da418d57-2468-5fdc-87b4-fdc98be18a66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064261 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-78eccfd7-1b8e-5e65-93e9-1255565406bf" "question-2b8242a3-946d-5b5e-b1f5-13dafc162222"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-78eccfd7-1b8e-5e65-93e9-1255565406bf" :question/id #uuid "78eccfd7-1b8e-5e65-93e9-1255565406bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-83553" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbb0eea61f7b4a4c6aa9f9b06462c327c9c6a366b5ff0ca21378f9be888f5ede.png)\n\nWhat is the total finite area enclosed between the curves $x=f(y)=3{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}$ and $x=g(y)=3y+6,$ as shown above?  \n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 3{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2} & =3y+6 \\\\ {y}^{3}+2{y}^{2} & =y+2 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y+2)-(y+2) & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-1)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,-1,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/aa/aa11be3a96d2055899bd17d700f1e59e7715bae2550d60d5c3a05d9f999b4008.png)\n\n \n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $y\\in [-2,-1],$ and the right function is $f(y)$ while the left function is $g(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}[f(y)-g(y)]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(3{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-3y-6)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{4}+2{y}^{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}-6y)|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4}-2-\\frac{3}{2}+6)-(12-16-6+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $y\\in [-1,1],$ and the right function is $g(y)$ while the left function is $f(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(g(y)-f(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3y+6-3{y}^{3}-6{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+6y-\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{4}-2{y}^{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+6-\\frac{3}{4}-2)-(\\frac{3}{2}-6-\\frac{3}{4}+2) \\\\  & =(4+\\frac{3}{4})-(\\frac{3}{4}-4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{5}{4}+8=\\frac{37}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8f2f1e55-4f59-572e-847b-dcb391381c6d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8f2f1e55-4f59-572e-847b-dcb391381c6d" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f2f1e55-4f59-572e-847b-dcb391381c6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-57e7febd-cf7c-58da-b9bb-2214d53efc34" :answer-field/choices ["answer-181b08a8-e848-5de5-baed-41104e58e10a" "answer-63047939-2514-5498-ae22-e5669ff0a0e4" "answer-57e7febd-cf7c-58da-b9bb-2214d53efc34" "answer-3224ae77-c3e6-58c2-a98f-a3a186e52051" "answer-03ee93b2-cfeb-50ed-aafc-620909d895c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-181b08a8-e848-5de5-baed-41104e58e10a" :answer/id #uuid "181b08a8-e848-5de5-baed-41104e58e10a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{19}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63047939-2514-5498-ae22-e5669ff0a0e4" :answer/id #uuid "63047939-2514-5498-ae22-e5669ff0a0e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57e7febd-cf7c-58da-b9bb-2214d53efc34" :answer/id #uuid "57e7febd-cf7c-58da-b9bb-2214d53efc34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{37}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3224ae77-c3e6-58c2-a98f-a3a186e52051" :answer/id #uuid "3224ae77-c3e6-58c2-a98f-a3a186e52051" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{41}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03ee93b2-cfeb-50ed-aafc-620909d895c8" :answer/id #uuid "03ee93b2-cfeb-50ed-aafc-620909d895c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064260 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-250e2076-51f3-53c1-af8d-a5206b051376" "question-ee79ba2d-b35e-5476-9b95-72d6c95d61d0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-250e2076-51f3-53c1-af8d-a5206b051376" :question/id #uuid "250e2076-51f3-53c1-af8d-a5206b051376" :question/math-academy-id "q-351774" :question/problem "![](images/2c/2cd4f059ef78b3ce7d6043dc216494340c08eb43edeab9b3869cd733c2f6d144.png)\n\nFind the total finite area $A$ enclosed between the curves $y=f(x)={x}^{3}-13x-10$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2.$ First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{3}-13x-10 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-13x-12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=-1$ is a root of ${x}^{3}-13x-12,$ and if we divide ${x}^{3}-13x-12$ by $x+1$ using synthetic division, we get ${x}^{2}-x-12.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)({x}^{2}-x-12) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-4)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,-1,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c4181d72f44f0190d03fa4239718e4f2877fda19a7b1f87421ef2746e27bcbc.png)\n\n  \n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $x\\in [-3,-1],$ and the upper function is $f(x)$ while the lower function is $g(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(f(x)-g(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(({x}^{3}-13x-10)-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}({x}^{3}-13x-12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{x}^{2}-12x){|}_{-3}^{-1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{(-1)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}(-1)^{2}-12(-1))-(\\frac{(-3)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}(-3)^{2}-12(-3)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}+12)-(\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{117}{2}+36) \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{4}-(-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $x\\in [-1,4],$ and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{4}(g(x)-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{4}(2-({x}^{3}-13x-10))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{4}({x}^{3}-13x-12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{x}^{2}-12x){|}_{-1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{{4}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}({4}^{2})-12(4))+(\\frac{(-1)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}(-1)^{2}-12(-1)) \\\\  & =-(64-104-48)+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}+12) \\\\  & =-(-88)+(\\frac{23}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{375}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=8+\\frac{375}{4}=\\frac{407}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-25149fb3-c68d-59af-b172-4e169f2ea0c6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-25149fb3-c68d-59af-b172-4e169f2ea0c6" :answer-field/id #uuid "25149fb3-c68d-59af-b172-4e169f2ea0c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cad2650d-fc76-528a-9adf-020467802be3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cad2650d-fc76-528a-9adf-020467802be3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cad2650d-fc76-528a-9adf-020467802be3" :answer/id #uuid "cad2650d-fc76-528a-9adf-020467802be3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{407}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2b8242a3-946d-5b5e-b1f5-13dafc162222" :question/id #uuid "2b8242a3-946d-5b5e-b1f5-13dafc162222" :question/math-academy-id "q-351822" :question/problem "![](images/87/87ce042a004af61a206bcef79711935dffb1b00d7157989532914ed355c007e1.png)\n\nWhat is the total finite area $A$ enclosed between the curves $x=-2{y}^{1/3}$ and $x=-\\frac{y}{8},$ as shown above? Do not round the answer.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(y)=-2{y}^{1/3}$ and $g(y)=-\\frac{y}{8}.$ First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ -2{y}^{1/3} & =-\\frac{y}{8} \\\\ 16{y}^{1/3} & =y \\\\ 4096y & ={y}^{3} \\\\ y({y}^{2}-4096) & =0 \\\\ y(y-64)(y+64) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce1c5d7fe654b406671eebf60eb65af5e11e20bb83adc08d81f6afca14856228.png)\n\n \n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $y\\in [-64,0],$ and the right function is $f(y)$ while the left function is $g(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-64}^{0}(f(y)-g(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-64}^{0}(-2{y}^{1/3}-(-\\frac{y}{8}))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-64}^{0}(-2{y}^{1/3}+\\frac{y}{8})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{3{y}^{4/3}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})|}_{-64}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-384+256) \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $y\\in [0,64],$ and the right function is $g(y)$ while the left function is $f(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}(g(y)-f(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}(-\\frac{y}{8}-(-2{y}^{1/3}))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}(-\\frac{y}{8}+2{y}^{1/3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{3{y}^{4/3}}{2})|}_{0}^{64} \\\\  & =(-256+384)-0 \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=128+128=256.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a9f6404-d0d1-555c-b66d-86ac7d693415"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a9f6404-d0d1-555c-b66d-86ac7d693415" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a9f6404-d0d1-555c-b66d-86ac7d693415" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-662ac86f-4640-5378-8ff2-bbb6d5c6c462" :answer-field/choices ["answer-662ac86f-4640-5378-8ff2-bbb6d5c6c462"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-662ac86f-4640-5378-8ff2-bbb6d5c6c462" :answer/id #uuid "662ac86f-4640-5378-8ff2-bbb6d5c6c462" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "256" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ee79ba2d-b35e-5476-9b95-72d6c95d61d0" :question/id #uuid "ee79ba2d-b35e-5476-9b95-72d6c95d61d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-353285" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6cd07ff0c0f5b1b092e6501f41e9f61f3f98e794b5075461acfb1e97309cb32.png)\n\nFind the total finite area $A$ enclosed between the curves $y=15{x}^{4}+1$ and $y=15{x}^{2}+1,$ as shown above.  Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=15{x}^{4}+1$ and $g(x)=15{x}^{2}+1.$ First, we find the intersections of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & f(x)=g(x) \\\\  & 15{x}^{4}+1=15{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & 15{x}^{4}=15{x}^{2} \\\\  & 15{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2}=0 \\\\  & 15{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-1)=0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/66/66ae463184e8d5fc9f667019be7fc5c893aea736a6d0dad8fa6a2619df9f08e8.png)\n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $x\\in [-1,0],$ and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(g(x)-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}((15{x}^{2}+1)-(15{x}^{4}+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(15{x}^{2}-15{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{5})|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(5(-1)^{3}-3(-1)^{5}) \\\\  & =0-(-5+3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $x\\in [0,1],$ and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(g(x)-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((15{x}^{2}+1)-(15{x}^{4}+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{x}^{2}-15{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{5})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(5-3)-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=2+2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-340857ff-4276-54b0-8e43-52c97591e55f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-340857ff-4276-54b0-8e43-52c97591e55f" :answer-field/id #uuid "340857ff-4276-54b0-8e43-52c97591e55f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2464e71d-94a2-53db-a635-1332ed3b3b62" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2464e71d-94a2-53db-a635-1332ed3b3b62"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2464e71d-94a2-53db-a635-1332ed3b3b62" :answer/id #uuid "2464e71d-94a2-53db-a635-1332ed3b3b62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-1ad6236a-078f-5f1f-ae8e-f7d60af63ee7" :activity/id #uuid "1ad6236a-078f-5f1f-ae8e-f7d60af63ee7" :activity/title "Finding Areas Between Curves that Intersect at More Than Two Points" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048771 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-f67ba52f-1056-572e-9b04-a2b486e7e734" "step-58e50150-93f6-5924-840c-44b85199257a" "step-15edc486-82c2-5128-8bf5-0227a35deb09" "step-3751a9bd-d099-5d27-83e4-6f3b2b251fee" "step-db9f3360-dfbc-5056-bcf9-dcdc3020d117"] :activity/first-step "step-f67ba52f-1056-572e-9b04-a2b486e7e734" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f67ba52f-1056-572e-9b04-a2b486e7e734" :step/id #uuid "f67ba52f-1056-572e-9b04-a2b486e7e734" :step/content "question-94f0b932-fd80-552e-9cee-96a821a87f03" :step/next "step-58e50150-93f6-5924-840c-44b85199257a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-58e50150-93f6-5924-840c-44b85199257a" :step/id #uuid "58e50150-93f6-5924-840c-44b85199257a" :step/content "question-78eccfd7-1b8e-5e65-93e9-1255565406bf" :step/next "step-15edc486-82c2-5128-8bf5-0227a35deb09" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-15edc486-82c2-5128-8bf5-0227a35deb09" :step/id #uuid "15edc486-82c2-5128-8bf5-0227a35deb09" :step/content "question-250e2076-51f3-53c1-af8d-a5206b051376" :step/next "step-3751a9bd-d099-5d27-83e4-6f3b2b251fee" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3751a9bd-d099-5d27-83e4-6f3b2b251fee" :step/id #uuid "3751a9bd-d099-5d27-83e4-6f3b2b251fee" :step/content "question-2b8242a3-946d-5b5e-b1f5-13dafc162222" :step/next "step-db9f3360-dfbc-5056-bcf9-dcdc3020d117" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-db9f3360-dfbc-5056-bcf9-dcdc3020d117" :step/id #uuid "db9f3360-dfbc-5056-bcf9-dcdc3020d117" :step/content "question-ee79ba2d-b35e-5476-9b95-72d6c95d61d0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064210 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3c5b79b6-c034-52ce-8e7b-d57a11d334d9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064209 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-faa87228-4c67-51da-9af8-97fb8de0d515"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064493 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-1/3}^{0}9x\\sqrt{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3x+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{1}{3}(u-1).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-1/3$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}9x\\sqrt{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{-1/3}^{0}\\frac{u-1}{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{5/2}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{3/2}{u}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {1}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3/2})-(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {0}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186061188 :answer-field/correct 17592186061193 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-faa87228-4c67-51da-9af8-97fb8de0d515" :question/id #uuid "faa87228-4c67-51da-9af8-97fb8de0d515" :question/math-academy-id "q-13190" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}x{(1+x)}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=1+x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=u-1.$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{(1+x)}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(u-1){u}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({u}^{5}-{u}^{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}-\\frac{1}{5}{u}^{5})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({2}^{6}-{1}^{6})-\\frac{1}{5}({2}^{5}-{1}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(64-1)-\\frac{1}{5}(32-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{6}-\\frac{31}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{31}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff7568f3-47d5-56fb-a003-0872e000e58b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff7568f3-47d5-56fb-a003-0872e000e58b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff7568f3-47d5-56fb-a003-0872e000e58b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5ce8cb92-d753-507f-8961-5d82ee267410" :answer-field/choices ["answer-55366fad-0795-57d6-85e8-4f68957d0932" "answer-88a10e65-44ad-55c3-b17c-5d31a83466d5" "answer-380d9a49-c6d8-5c6c-aad5-cf5792818606" "answer-5ce8cb92-d753-507f-8961-5d82ee267410" "answer-be4996ff-8496-533e-9142-f9c34a17d2a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55366fad-0795-57d6-85e8-4f68957d0932" :answer/id #uuid "55366fad-0795-57d6-85e8-4f68957d0932" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88a10e65-44ad-55c3-b17c-5d31a83466d5" :answer/id #uuid "88a10e65-44ad-55c3-b17c-5d31a83466d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{31}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-380d9a49-c6d8-5c6c-aad5-cf5792818606" :answer/id #uuid "380d9a49-c6d8-5c6c-aad5-cf5792818606" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{39}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ce8cb92-d753-507f-8961-5d82ee267410" :answer/id #uuid "5ce8cb92-d753-507f-8961-5d82ee267410" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{43}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be4996ff-8496-533e-9142-f9c34a17d2a5" :answer/id #uuid "be4996ff-8496-533e-9142-f9c34a17d2a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{61}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3c5b79b6-c034-52ce-8e7b-d57a11d334d9" :question/id #uuid "3c5b79b6-c034-52ce-8e7b-d57a11d334d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-49530" :question/problem "Given that $u=x+3$, which integral is equivalent to $\\int x\\sqrt[4]{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u=x+3$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n$x=u-3.$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt[4]{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (u-3)(\\sqrt[4]{u})(\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int (u-3)({u}^{1/4})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int ({u}^{5/4}-3{u}^{1/4})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f1104bd1-f1f5-5680-a66e-cd85a4936d91"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f1104bd1-f1f5-5680-a66e-cd85a4936d91" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1104bd1-f1f5-5680-a66e-cd85a4936d91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3b263d64-e19f-5c23-b3db-3e186e391929" :answer-field/choices ["answer-224c5398-c047-573f-95f0-b412ddff5a5f" "answer-5a6ac55b-549f-597d-a76b-816ff07700f5" "answer-3b263d64-e19f-5c23-b3db-3e186e391929" "answer-a394660e-4646-564e-9eaf-6f37082eeb48" "answer-29884f8a-7e32-5907-a04f-c79f0b1e1354"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-224c5398-c047-573f-95f0-b412ddff5a5f" :answer/id #uuid "224c5398-c047-573f-95f0-b412ddff5a5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int ({u}^{1/4}-3{u}^{-1/4})\\text{d}u" 