ulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066330 :answer-field/correct "answer-8b3e1513-7c61-5e21-83f2-a7a8ded3d445" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b3e1513-7c61-5e21-83f2-a7a8ded3d445"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b3e1513-7c61-5e21-83f2-a7a8ded3d445" :answer/id #uuid "8b3e1513-7c61-5e21-83f2-a7a8ded3d445" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066329 :answer-field/correct "answer-7d875ff3-47eb-58c1-b1ae-67c39fc7ba60" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d875ff3-47eb-58c1-b1ae-67c39fc7ba60"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d875ff3-47eb-58c1-b1ae-67c39fc7ba60" :answer/id #uuid "7d875ff3-47eb-58c1-b1ae-67c39fc7ba60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186400937 :question/problem "If the expression ${y}^{2}-10y+30$ is written as $(y+p)^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5)^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7bec21b0-2997-5975-9f9b-3c9e1dd0a0f7" :question/id #uuid "7bec21b0-2997-5975-9f9b-3c9e1dd0a0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-250468" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-8y+26.$\n\n${y}^{2}-8y+26=(${{field-1}}$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+26\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $26$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{4}^{2}+26\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+26\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}+10.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-acac12fd-6050-55d3-9678-5af612254a1a" "answer-field-0022224a-fdbd-54de-967f-2328c3eb6871"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-acac12fd-6050-55d3-9678-5af612254a1a" :answer-field/id #uuid "acac12fd-6050-55d3-9678-5af612254a1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-420966e2-15ca-55c3-9f09-b53c223be7c1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-420966e2-15ca-55c3-9f09-b53c223be7c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-420966e2-15ca-55c3-9f09-b53c223be7c1" :answer/id #uuid "420966e2-15ca-55c3-9f09-b53c223be7c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0022224a-fdbd-54de-967f-2328c3eb6871" :answer-field/id #uuid "0022224a-fdbd-54de-967f-2328c3eb6871" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-80303f1c-e488-559a-b935-80446c88791a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-80303f1c-e488-559a-b935-80446c88791a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80303f1c-e488-559a-b935-80446c88791a" :answer/id #uuid "80303f1c-e488-559a-b935-80446c88791a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-93dc778c-0905-5be0-ac96-c8581ce3887e" :question/id #uuid "93dc778c-0905-5be0-ac96-c8581ce3887e" :question/math-academy-id "q-3661" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-12y+15.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 6y+15\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $15$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+15\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+15\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-6)^{2}-21.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4962f429-26e5-56ce-9a63-ab7afa5c8c95"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4962f429-26e5-56ce-9a63-ab7afa5c8c95" :answer-field/id #uuid "4962f429-26e5-56ce-9a63-ab7afa5c8c95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5e016c46-3617-5f9d-94b5-1c43f2e69e06" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a4c82222-c3ad-57fc-b6a2-8ef786ebfe1b" "answer-5743c63c-942a-5f21-8bac-7ec193c1e106" "answer-5e016c46-3617-5f9d-94b5-1c43f2e69e06" "answer-f603e514-ee50-5297-9060-98ad3751aeb1" "answer-1932a79d-4e2f-5d3f-a617-e8ffcb8d71aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4c82222-c3ad-57fc-b6a2-8ef786ebfe1b" :answer/id #uuid "a4c82222-c3ad-57fc-b6a2-8ef786ebfe1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-12)^{2}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5743c63c-942a-5f21-8bac-7ec193c1e106" :answer/id #uuid "5743c63c-942a-5f21-8bac-7ec193c1e106" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-6)^{2}+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e016c46-3617-5f9d-94b5-1c43f2e69e06" :answer/id #uuid "5e016c46-3617-5f9d-94b5-1c43f2e69e06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-6)^{2}-21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f603e514-ee50-5297-9060-98ad3751aeb1" :answer/id #uuid "f603e514-ee50-5297-9060-98ad3751aeb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-6)^{2}-25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1932a79d-4e2f-5d3f-a617-e8ffcb8d71aa" :answer/id #uuid "1932a79d-4e2f-5d3f-a617-e8ffcb8d71aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-6)^{2}-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068977 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186205310 :answer-field/choices 17592186205312] [:db/retract 17592186205310 :answer-field/choices 17592186205313] [:db/retract 17592186205310 :answer-field/choices 17592186205314] [:db/retract 17592186205310 :answer-field/choices 17592186205311] [:db/retract 17592186205335 :answer-field/choices 17592186205336] [:db/retract 17592186205335 :answer-field/choices 17592186205337] [:db/retract 17592186205335 :answer-field/choices 17592186205338] [:db/retract 17592186205335 :answer-field/choices 17592186205339] [:db/retract 17592186205335 :answer-field/choices 17592186205340] {:db/id 17592186208846 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-66d888b2-986e-58dd-b75b-3ffbf568a4ee" "question-81dfe0ee-3be8-54e9-80f8-e08132e662d6" "question-45fa10fd-570c-5dfa-b523-d7d0d1d7035b" "question-fd553fc9-eb2f-5541-8477-0b92e94ae292"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186208271 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by ![](images/47/47c73d44f28380c2285b914445d6e4eed32e20e4304f8373b0e650d238497569.svg) with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ at ![](images/06/0648ff5db79fc2b00307e99fc1b14ddbc277982db84205f281f567a9c39b1eeb.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Writing ![](images/f6/f6bba4e49e37c8115f3d5696f3763224ae83da30fa92e295da8ae2905b6cfe9a.svg), we have\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x})= \\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{x}) = 2A\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n![](images/b2/b21fb5c13d179b77b77ba08ccd62b4e7f47274fac9fc9ea8c39936a9e52ddcef.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186208847 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6620062e-023f-5c7d-9835-fb1b352d0fc1" "question-a1a89719-12d3-55da-8498-af0dfd930411" "question-4ab946d8-2af3-5f4a-a52a-5bbd914240e1" "question-9de18b18-ac36-55ed-9eeb-ad23a6b9ac98"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186208289 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by\n![](images/9e/9e4b8dccd90c15d35317eb418a99dde9c43f12b8b2780e35292ecc291c10e92d.svg)\nwith respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ at  ![](images/37/37e7e5c54e3e7990b627649376f8f55a27db8a82b42b2375f99941793c92ff26.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n![](images/71/7135a764f77153e7753bd3ab87305f8347207f12d87533565c0018588e00a00f.svg)\nwe have $f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{x}) = (A^T + A)\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n![](images/e5/e5e95b87bd905379b447b17e1ba8946579b85b6ab29831912c154591d5d7bde4.svg)\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at ![](images/5b/5b3d05513f25be9f2d0cc61ba21dbf3b34916f6c2fce1babc8fb4de1627171da.svg)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+0 \\\\ 0-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186208848 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-41cc5d66-bf23-517e-b32a-3cd7a74b1b32" "question-d5a5695b-8296-5985-af65-b20134a9d8cb" "question-411aabaa-bd7a-5b74-b0d5-19adca16cb89" "question-c92e05a7-340f-573b-9fe1-3137e228de5f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186208272 :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{3} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $L(\\mathbf{x}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})^T(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$ where \n![](images/38/38fae06690f00ec57f1c56f5b3c0f4add88d5505a991f7cbd09020364c973238.svg)\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} L(\\mathbf{x})$ at ![](images/62/6267a6f3c8df9f11cfa395a92d426ab1901e9048cadc6b50640fc5aafc0b59df.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}.$ Then, $L(\\mathbf{x}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y},$ and\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} L = \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}L}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}}\\right)^T = \n\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}}\\right)^T \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}L}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}\\right)^T.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y}\\right) = \\mathbf{y}^T \n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}) = \\mathbf{A},\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ![](images/41/41633d52787dbf98d039bf414f07a7052fe0acf0b55c831bbaf24868b22e95df.svg), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 81 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186208924 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205310 :answer-field/correct "answer-e1fcfb1c-90f2-5d7d-a157-d8f5b54b8b5d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7e6596af-03f0-55a8-b320-a0ebb705a39f" "answer-1a124c13-f554-5359-962c-90f7cd894e01" "answer-23346f1c-daf0-53aa-8167-87d06065ca2a" "answer-e1fcfb1c-90f2-5d7d-a157-d8f5b54b8b5d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e6596af-03f0-55a8-b320-a0ebb705a39f" :answer/id #uuid "7e6596af-03f0-55a8-b320-a0ebb705a39f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a124c13-f554-5359-962c-90f7cd894e01" :answer/id #uuid "1a124c13-f554-5359-962c-90f7cd894e01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23346f1c-daf0-53aa-8167-87d06065ca2a" :answer/id #uuid "23346f1c-daf0-53aa-8167-87d06065ca2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1fcfb1c-90f2-5d7d-a157-d8f5b54b8b5d" :answer/id #uuid "e1fcfb1c-90f2-5d7d-a157-d8f5b54b8b5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-66d888b2-986e-58dd-b75b-3ffbf568a4ee" :question/id #uuid "66d888b2-986e-58dd-b75b-3ffbf568a4ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-265764" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fd1b93f4-6b48-5f90-af0f-e979b93d3583"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fd1b93f4-6b48-5f90-af0f-e979b93d3583" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd1b93f4-6b48-5f90-af0f-e979b93d3583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5de37790-75ca-5675-aae7-0ff1b0d1659e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b296bdc-8919-5ab6-827d-d951a9c46e17" "answer-c4108243-e1bd-51f1-bef8-13c020e0ba37" "answer-5de37790-75ca-5675-aae7-0ff1b0d1659e" "answer-87e4df02-a07b-51ac-ba3c-6c80acabaddf" "answer-b1503f40-d92c-57b6-99d1-6c5dcdf41eb9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b296bdc-8919-5ab6-827d-d951a9c46e17" :answer/id #uuid "7b296bdc-8919-5ab6-827d-d951a9c46e17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :ans